= Argument — Technical =

== Abstract form ==
An argument can be represented as:

<pre>
Γ
∴ φ
</pre>

where Γ is a set or sequence of premises and φ is the conclusion.

== Evaluation standards ==
Different argument classes require different norms:

* deductive validity;
* inductive strength;
* probabilistic support;
* explanatory adequacy;
* defeasible warrant;
* relevance and dialectical burden.

Applying deductive validity to every argument type is a category mistake.

== Argument versus inference ==
An argument is a representational structure.

An inference can be the act or relation of moving from premises/evidence to a conclusion.

One inference may be represented by several argument forms.

== Argument versus explanation ==
Explanation and argument can share propositions while differing pragmatically in what is taken as given and what is at issue.

